1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,279
What does a double integral actually mean? And why does changing coordinates suddenly introduce a

2
00:00:07,279 --> 00:00:14,408
mysterious extra factor? This chapter is one idea seen from several angles. Break a region into

3
00:00:14,408 --> 00:00:21,162
tiny pieces. Work out what each piece contributes. Then add everything. We will build that

4
00:00:21,162 --> 00:00:28,441
picture, learn to read integration bounds from a drawing, and watch the polar and spherical scale

5
00:00:28,441 --> 00:00:35,720
factors emerge from geometry. Keep asking two questions: what are the pieces, and how big is each

6
00:00:35,720 --> 00:00:36,020
one?

7
00:00:37,833 --> 00:00:44,335
Imagine a landscape whose height above the floor is f of x and y. Cover the floor with a

8
00:00:44,335 --> 00:00:51,501
rectangular grid. Above each little tile, build a column reaching up to the landscape. Its volume

9
00:00:51,501 --> 00:00:58,741
is height times base area. The blue tile tells us where we sample. The yellow column tells us what

10
00:00:58,741 --> 00:01:05,464
we add. At first this is only an approximation. A coarse column misses some of the changing

11
00:01:05,464 --> 00:01:07,163
surface above its tile.

12
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:16,152
Now shrink the tiles. The staircase of columns follows the surface more closely. For a continuous

13
00:01:16,152 --> 00:01:22,861
function on this rectangle, the sums converge to a single number. That number is the double

14
00:01:22,861 --> 00:01:29,644
integral. Notice that the two integral signs describe a two dimensional collection of sample

15
00:01:29,644 --> 00:01:36,648
points. They do not mean we are squaring an ordinary integral. If the function is negative, its

16
00:01:36,648 --> 00:01:43,800
contribution is negative too. Only a nonnegative height gives ordinary geometric volume directly.

17
00:01:45,625 --> 00:01:53,655
We cannot literally add infinitely many columns one by one. Instead, group them into strips.

18
00:01:53,655 --> 00:02:01,161
Freeze x. Within that strip, integrate along y from zero to one. Since x is fixed, its

19
00:02:01,161 --> 00:02:09,279
contribution is simply x times a length of one. Integrating y contributes one half. The strip

20
00:02:09,279 --> 00:02:17,745
therefore has cross sectional area x plus one half. Now integrate those areas as x runs from zero

21
00:02:17,745 --> 00:02:23,855
to two. The result is three cubic units. This is an iterated integral.

22
00:02:25,667 --> 00:02:32,328
We could group the same columns into rows running the other way. For continuous functions on a

23
00:02:32,328 --> 00:02:39,273
rectangle, Fubini's theorem says both orders give the same total. The inner differential tells you

24
00:02:39,273 --> 00:02:45,863
which direction to sweep first. Read from the inside outward. Switching the order can turn an

25
00:02:45,863 --> 00:02:52,383
awkward calculation into an easy one. But this is a theorem with hypotheses. Continuity is a

26
00:02:52,383 --> 00:02:58,903
useful sufficient condition here; do not assume every improper integral permits an arbitrary

27
00:02:58,903 --> 00:03:00,037
change of order.

28
00:03:01,875 --> 00:03:09,572
Rectangles are friendly because every strip has the same endpoints. Real regions often curve. Look

29
00:03:09,572 --> 00:03:16,877
at the lens between y equals x squared and y equals x. Move a vertical scan line from left to

30
00:03:16,877 --> 00:03:24,574
right. Each time, it enters at the parabola and leaves at the line. That observation is the entire

31
00:03:24,574 --> 00:03:32,193
setup. The outer bounds are zero to one. The inner bounds are x squared to x. The bounds encode a

32
00:03:32,193 --> 00:03:35,335
picture, rather than a rule to memorize.

33
00:03:37,167 --> 00:03:44,788
Now sweep horizontally. At a fixed height y, the left edge lies on x equals y. The right edge lies

34
00:03:44,788 --> 00:03:52,409
on x equals the square root of y. These are the same two curves solved for x. The total region has

35
00:03:52,409 --> 00:03:59,874
not changed, but the description has. With height one, either order gives the area: the integral

36
00:03:59,874 --> 00:04:06,562
from zero to one of x minus x squared, which is one sixth. A good check is to draw one

37
00:04:06,562 --> 00:04:10,217
representative strip and verify both endpoints.

38
00:04:12,042 --> 00:04:19,819
A circular region fights a rectangular grid. So let the grid bend into rings and spokes. One tiny

39
00:04:19,819 --> 00:04:27,596
tile has radial thickness d r. Its other side is a short circular arc. Arc length is radius times

40
00:04:27,596 --> 00:04:35,214
angle, so that side has length r d theta. Multiply the two sides and you get r d r d theta. The

41
00:04:35,214 --> 00:04:42,590
extra r is not an algebraic tax. Farther from the origin, equally spaced spokes enclose more

42
00:04:42,590 --> 00:04:47,802
physical area. The differential must account for that stretching.

43
00:04:49,625 --> 00:04:56,831
Consider the dome z equals one minus x squared minus y squared, above the unit disk. In polar

44
00:04:56,831 --> 00:05:04,038
coordinates its height is one minus r squared. The radius runs from zero to one and the angle

45
00:05:04,038 --> 00:05:11,477
makes one complete turn. Each little contribution is height times polar tile area. Integrating r

46
00:05:11,477 --> 00:05:18,761
minus r cubed gives one quarter, and the full turn multiplies that by two pi. The volume is pi

47
00:05:18,761 --> 00:05:24,495
over two. The symmetry has made both the region and the integrand simpler.

48
00:05:26,333 --> 00:05:33,423
The floor tile need not carry a height. It might carry density. If delta is mass per unit area,

49
00:05:33,423 --> 00:05:40,662
then delta times tile area is a tiny mass. Add those masses to get the mass of a thin sheet. If f

50
00:05:40,662 --> 00:05:47,602
represents temperature instead, the integral sums temperature weighted by area. Divide by the

51
00:05:47,602 --> 00:05:54,841
total area to obtain the average temperature. The operation is the same; the meaning and units of

52
00:05:54,841 --> 00:05:58,273
the integrand change what the answer measures.

53
00:06:00,083 --> 00:06:06,971
To find a balance point, weight each position by the mass sitting there. The x coordinate of the

54
00:06:06,971 --> 00:06:13,859
center of mass is the integral of x times density, divided by total mass. The y coordinate works

55
00:06:13,859 --> 00:06:20,818
the same way. Here density grows toward the right, so the balance point shifts right. On the unit

56
00:06:20,818 --> 00:06:27,562
square with density one plus x, the mass is three halves and the center is at five ninths, one

57
00:06:27,562 --> 00:06:34,235
half. Moments of inertia use squared distance instead: mass farther from an axis matters much

58
00:06:34,235 --> 00:06:34,593
more.

59
00:06:36,417 --> 00:06:43,439
A probability density uses exactly the same machinery. A small tile carries approximately density

60
00:06:43,439 --> 00:06:50,245
times tile area of probability. Integrating over a region gives the chance that a random point

61
00:06:50,245 --> 00:06:57,340
lands inside it. The density must be nonnegative and its integral over all possible positions must

62
00:06:57,340 --> 00:07:04,146
be one. A density value itself can exceed one; the probability of a region cannot. Likewise, a

63
00:07:04,146 --> 00:07:11,024
moment of inertia integrates squared distance to an axis times mass density. The geometry tells

64
00:07:11,024 --> 00:07:16,237
you the pieces, and the integrand tells you what each piece contributes.

65
00:07:18,042 --> 00:07:24,996
A different question: how much material would you need to cover the landscape itself? That is

66
00:07:24,996 --> 00:07:32,101
surface area, rather than the volume underneath. A small patch projects to a tile on the floor,

67
00:07:32,101 --> 00:07:39,429
but tilting makes the surface patch larger than its shadow. Near a point, replace the surface with

68
00:07:39,429 --> 00:07:46,683
its tangent plane. Two tangent edges are represented by the vectors one, zero, f sub x, and zero,

69
00:07:46,683 --> 00:07:53,787
one, f sub y. Their cross product has length the square root of one plus f sub x squared plus f

70
00:07:53,787 --> 00:07:57,452
sub y squared. This is the local area multiplier.

71
00:07:59,292 --> 00:08:05,957
For a concrete example, take the plane z equals x plus y above the unit square. Its slope in each

72
00:08:05,957 --> 00:08:12,278
coordinate direction is one. Therefore every shadow tile corresponds to a surface patch with

73
00:08:12,278 --> 00:08:18,943
square root of three times as much area. Integrate that constant factor over the unit square: the

74
00:08:18,943 --> 00:08:25,676
surface area is square root of three. If the surface were horizontal, both slopes would vanish and

75
00:08:25,676 --> 00:08:32,273
the multiplier would become one. This surface stretching is closely related to the Jacobian idea

76
00:08:32,273 --> 00:08:33,922
we will meet at the end.

77
00:08:35,750 --> 00:08:43,271
For a solid region, replace floor tiles with tiny boxes. Their volume is d x times d y times d z.

78
00:08:43,271 --> 00:08:50,714
Integrating one counts volume. Integrating a volume density counts mass. The third integral sign

79
00:08:50,714 --> 00:08:58,080
means the sample points now fill a three dimensional region. To choose bounds, start with a box

80
00:08:58,080 --> 00:09:05,678
somewhere inside the solid. Trace the innermost direction until you hit the two bounding surfaces.

81
00:09:05,678 --> 00:09:10,330
Then describe where the remaining two coordinates can range.

82
00:09:12,167 --> 00:09:19,951
Take the tetrahedron in the positive octant below x plus y plus z equals one. At a fixed x and y,

83
00:09:19,951 --> 00:09:27,656
a vertical column starts on z equals zero and stops at z equals one minus x minus y. Project the

84
00:09:27,656 --> 00:09:35,360
solid onto the floor. That shadow is a triangle where x and y are nonnegative and x plus y is at

85
00:09:35,360 --> 00:09:43,064
most one. This gives y from zero to one minus x, and x from zero to one. The volume works out to

86
00:09:43,064 --> 00:09:43,867
one sixth.

87
00:09:45,667 --> 00:09:52,573
A cylinder suggests a polar grid on the floor and an ordinary vertical coordinate. Extrude each

88
00:09:52,573 --> 00:09:59,553
polar tile through a small height d z. Its volume is r d r d theta d z. Nothing new has happened

89
00:09:59,553 --> 00:10:06,677
to the stretching: it is still the widening of the spokes in the floor. For a cylinder of radius a

90
00:10:06,677 --> 00:10:13,366
and height h, integrating one gives pi a squared h. This is a useful check that the factor r

91
00:10:13,366 --> 00:10:15,547
belongs in the volume element.

92
00:10:17,375 --> 00:10:24,610
For a ball, even cylindrical coordinates leave a curved boundary. Spherical coordinates measure

93
00:10:24,610 --> 00:10:31,845
distance rho from the origin, angle theta around the vertical axis, and angle phi down from the

94
00:10:31,845 --> 00:10:38,928
positive z axis. This last convention matters. Phi is the polar angle, not latitude above the

95
00:10:38,928 --> 00:10:46,391
equator. A full ball has rho from zero to its radius, theta from zero to two pi, and phi from zero

96
00:10:46,391 --> 00:10:51,265
to pi. Now look closely at one small block on a spherical shell.

97
00:10:53,083 --> 00:11:00,597
The block has three short edges. Its radial thickness is d rho. Moving down a meridian gives

98
00:11:00,597 --> 00:11:08,275
length rho d phi. Moving around the latitude circle gives length rho sine phi d theta, because

99
00:11:08,275 --> 00:11:16,034
that circle has radius rho sine phi. Multiply all three lengths. The result is rho squared sine

100
00:11:16,034 --> 00:11:23,875
phi times the three coordinate changes. Near the pole, latitude circles shrink. Near the equator

101
00:11:23,875 --> 00:11:29,183
they are largest. The sine factor captures exactly that geometry.

102
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:38,309
Integrate one over a ball of radius a using that volume element. The radial integral gives a cubed

103
00:11:38,309 --> 00:11:45,395
over three. The polar angle integral of sine gives two. The azimuthal angle contributes two pi.

104
00:11:45,395 --> 00:11:52,555
Together they give four pi a cubed over three. Forgetting the sine would count too much near the

105
00:11:52,555 --> 00:11:59,640
poles. Forgetting rho squared would fail to account for shells growing as you move outward. The

106
00:11:59,640 --> 00:12:04,190
familiar volume formula is a check on our tiny block picture.

107
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:12,660
Polar coordinates are one example of a general idea. Start with a tiny rectangle in a u v

108
00:12:12,660 --> 00:12:19,993
parameter plane. A smooth transformation sends it to an approximately parallelogram shaped tile in

109
00:12:19,993 --> 00:12:27,102
the x y plane. Its two edge directions come from the partial derivatives of the transformation.

110
00:12:27,102 --> 00:12:34,286
Their determinant measures signed area scaling. We take the absolute value because physical area

111
00:12:34,286 --> 00:12:40,946
is nonnegative. This determinant is the Jacobian. It is the local conversion rate between

112
00:12:40,946 --> 00:12:43,490
coordinate area and physical area.

113
00:12:45,292 --> 00:12:52,581
For example, stretch a unit disk by a factor of two horizontally. The map x equals two u, y equals

114
00:12:52,581 --> 00:12:59,796
v turns the disk into an ellipse. Every tiny area doubles, so the Jacobian is two and the ellipse

115
00:12:59,796 --> 00:13:06,490
has area two pi. In general, transform both the region and the integrand, then include the

116
00:13:06,490 --> 00:13:13,185
absolute Jacobian. Use a transformation that covers the region once, apart from negligible

117
00:13:13,185 --> 00:13:20,325
boundaries, and is locally invertible in its interior. Otherwise you may accidentally count some

118
00:13:20,325 --> 00:13:21,962
points more than once.

119
00:13:23,792 --> 00:13:31,199
Here is the workflow to carry into the exercises. Sketch the region first. Choose coordinates that

120
00:13:31,199 --> 00:13:38,607
fit its shape. Draw a representative tiny piece and identify its size. Read the bounds by sweeping

121
00:13:38,607 --> 00:13:45,939
that piece through the region. Then integrate from the inside outward. Check the sign, the units,

122
00:13:45,939 --> 00:13:53,119
and the scale of the result. Double integrals, triple integrals, and Jacobians are connected by

123
00:13:53,119 --> 00:14:00,527
this same picture: local contribution, times the size of a piece, added over the whole region. The

124
00:14:00,527 --> 00:14:03,702
symbols should now remind you of geometry.